{
  "id": "three-sum",
  "title": "3Sum",
  "difficulty": "medium",
  "categories": ["two-pointers", "sorting", "array"],
  "descriptionMd": "Given an integer array `nums`, find every **distinct triplet** of values `[a, b, c]` taken from three different positions of the array such that `a + b + c == 0`.\n\nTwo triplets count as the same if they contain the same values, so the answer must not contain duplicates. The triplets, and the numbers inside each triplet, may be returned in any order.\n\n**Example 1**\n\n```\nnums = [-1, 0, 1, 2, -1, -4]\n→ [[-1, -1, 2], [-1, 0, 1]]\n```\nThe value triplet `[-1, 0, 1]` can be formed in two ways, but it is listed once.\n\n**Example 2**\n\n```\nnums = [0, 1, 1]\n→ []\n```\nNo three values add up to zero.\n\n**Follow-up:** can you beat O(n³) and avoid using a set to remove duplicates?",
  "constraints": ["3 ≤ nums.length ≤ 3000", "-10⁵ ≤ nums[i] ≤ 10⁵"],
  "hints": ["If you fix the first value a, the problem becomes finding pairs that sum to -a.", "Sorting the array lets you find those pairs with two pointers moving inward.", "In a sorted array, equal values sit next to each other — skipping repeats is how you avoid duplicate triplets."],
  "signature": {
    "functionName": "threeSum",
    "params": [{ "name": "nums", "type": "int[]" }],
    "returns": "int[][]"
  },
  "comparator": "unorderedNested",
